.\" Rendered by make man-pages; staged on play image in /usr/man/.
.TH NULIB2 1 "September 21, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
nulib2 \- read a NuFX (ShrinkIt) archive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nulib2
.BR \-t | \-v
.I archive
.br
.B nulib2
.BR \-x | \-p | \-i
.I archive
.RI [ name ]
.br
.B nulib2
.B \-a
.I archive file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B \-t
lists the names in
.IR archive ,
one per line and nothing else, so the output can be fed to another
program.
.PP
.B \-v
lists them with their attributes, one line per record:
.PP
.RS
.nf
TTT SSSSSSSS $AAAA NAME
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I TTT
is the ProDOS file type, using
.BR ls (1)'s
own three-character rule \- TXT, BIN, DIR, SYS, BAS, or "$xx" for a
type with no mnemonic.
.I SSSSSSSS
is the size of the record's data fork in bytes, right-aligned in eight
columns, or "\-" for a record that carries no data fork.
.I $AAAA
is the auxiliary type.
Each row begins with one space; a record whose header CRC did not verify
begins with
.B !
instead.
.PP
.PP
.B \-x
writes each record's data fork into the current directory as a real
ProDOS file, with the record's own file type and auxiliary type \- not
the TXT a plain create would leave, because on ProDOS the type is where
"can this be run?" lives and maux loads a binary at its auxiliary type.
.PP
A record's pathname may name directories.
.B nulib2
does not create them: it writes the last component into the current
directory, splitting on the separator the record itself declares.
.PP
.B \-p
writes every fork to standard output instead, concatenated and
unadorned, so a single-file archive can be piped straight into
something else.
.PP
.B \-i
expands the whole archive and checks its checksums without writing
anything.
.PP
Any of
.BR \-x ", " \-p ", or " \-i
may take a
.I name
after
.IR archive :
only the record whose own name matches it is extracted, listed
against, or checked, instead of every record in the archive.
The name is compared as the record itself stores it \- with any
high\-bit "display" convention already stripped \- and is most useful
on
.B \-p
against a
.I .SDK
holding exactly one disk record, so the record's own bytes reach
standard output on their own rather than concatenated with anything
else the archive might also hold.
A
.I name
that matches nothing produces no output and no diagnostic;
.B \-t
and
.B \-v
do not take one.
.PP
An archive wrapped in a 128-byte Binary II header \- a
.I .BXY
\- is recognised and stepped over without being asked.
.PP
.B \-a
writes an archive holding the named files, each carrying its own
ProDOS type, auxiliary type and access byte.
Only the leaf of each name is stored; this does not create directory
records.
.PP
It CREATES, and does not add to an existing archive \- the name is
overwritten.
Adding means rebuilding: threads are compressed and variable-length,
so there is no unit to write through and every untouched record would
have to be re-emitted from its own bytes.
.PP
An operand of
.B \-
means standard input: no length is known ahead of time, so nothing
is read twice \-
.B \-0
is refused on it, and a record made from it that also ends up in a
.I .SDK
archive whose true length was not a multiple of 512 is written
anyway, the block count silently floored, rather than skipped.
Its record is named for
.IR archive 's
own leaf with any extension removed
.RI ( FLOP.SDK
becomes
.IR FLOP ),
since a pipe carries no name of its own; its type and access are the
same defaults an unreadable path already gets, and it carries no
useful date, since there is no clock call cheap enough to spend on
one bare timestamp.
.PP
When
.I archive
ends in
.RI . SDK
(case-insensitively), every operand is archived as a disk image
instead of a file: a record with a disk-image thread, sized in
512-byte blocks, holding the whole input.
An operand whose length is zero or not a multiple of 512 is skipped
with a warning, exactly as one that cannot be read is \- except a
.B \-
operand, per above.
.B \-v
shows such a record with
.I Disk
in place of its file type and the block count in place of its
auxiliary type.
This is how ShrinkIt and CiderPress already tell the two kinds of
archive apart; there is no flag for it; a real ShrinkIt-family tool
has none either, because NufxLib's own command line cannot add a
disk image at all.
.PP
Each fork is compressed in the
.I LZW/2
format, which is what GS/ShrinkIt writes and every ShrinkIt reads:
a run-length pass over each 4096-byte chunk, then LZW over the
result.
.PP
Every chunk is priced in all three of its legal shapes and written in
the cheapest \- LZW, run-length coded, or stored \- so a fork of
mixed content does not lose to its least compressible region, and
nothing ever comes out larger than it went in.
A chunk that is not LZW coded empties the string table, on this side
exactly as on the reader's.
.PP
The table is 4096 entries and its codes grow from nine bits to
twelve; neither side transmits the width, and when the table fills,
a clear code starts it again.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-t
List the names, one per line.
.TP
.B \-v
List the records with their attributes.
.TP
.B \-x
Extract into the current directory.
.TP
.B \-p
Extract to standard output.
.TP
.B \-i
Test integrity; write nothing.
.TP
.B \-a
Create
.I archive
holding the named files.
.PP
Exactly one is required.
A bare
.I archive
operand is a usage error rather than a guess, and so is naming two:
listing and extracting are two passes over a file this reader walks
once.
.SH "COMPRESSION AND WHAT IS NOT READ"
A NuFX archive compresses each
.I thread
separately, so one archive may mix formats.
This version parses every record and thread header whatever the format,
and reports sizes from the headers.
It can EXTRACT a
.I stored
thread, an
.I LZW/1
one (what ProDOS 8 ShrinkIt writes) and an
.I LZW/2
one (what GS/ShrinkIt writes) \- between them, every archive either
version of ShrinkIt produced.
Stored is less rare than it sounds: the reference implementation falls
back to storing whenever compressing a thread would make it bigger.
.PP
Squeeze, LZC, deflate and bzip2 are not expanded, and are out of scope
rather than missing: no version of ShrinkIt ever wrote any of them.
LZC is UNIX
.BR compress (1)
put into a NuFX archive by
.BR nulib (1)
on a Unix host, which no Apple II program could ever read back;
deflate and bzip2 are NufxLib extensions its own help describes as
unpackable on an Apple II.
A thread in any of those is named on standard error and skipped, and
the run exits 1.
.PP
Binary II wrappers
.RB ( .BXY )
are stepped over without being asked; see the description above.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 if the archive was listed and every CRC verified, 1 if
it could not be opened, ended early, or a CRC did not match, and 2 for
a usage error.
A CRC failure does not stop the listing \- a damaged record is exactly
when the rest of the file is worth seeing \- so a run can print every
row and still exit 1.
.PP
An LZW/1 thread carries a second checksum inside its own stream header,
over every chunk including the zero padding of the last one.
.B nulib2
verifies that as well, and reports a stream that fails it as damaged
rather than reporting its thread CRC \- which would only say the same
thing twice.
An LZW/2 thread has no such checksum; instead every compressed chunk
declares how many bytes its codes occupy, and
.B nulib2
refuses a chunk whose codes did not consume exactly that.
.PP
A fork whose CRC does not match is reported but KEPT: the bytes on disk
are the only copy anyone has, and a CRC mismatch says the archive
disagrees with itself, not that the data is worthless.
A thread CRC is only meaningful on a version 3 record; a version 1
record (what ProDOS 8 ShrinkIt writes) keeps its checksum inside the
compressed stream, so there is nothing at thread level to test and
.B nulib2
does not pretend otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.RS
.nf
nulib2 \-t /mnt/MAUX/GAME.SHK
cd /mnt/MAUX/WORK; nulib2 \-x /mnt/MAUX/GAME.SHK
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
/usr/man/NULIB2  this page
.SH CAVEATS
A verified CRC is not proof the data is correct.
A thread's CRC covers what the decoder produces from the stream the
encoder wrote, so an archive whose encoder was wrong is
self-consistently wrong and verifies clean.
This is not hypothetical: the reference implementation's own RLE
encoder corrupts runs of its escape byte, and the resulting archives
pass their own integrity check.
Verify against the original file, never against the archive alone.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chtyp (1),
.BR ls (1)
.SH HISTORY
NuFX is the archive format of Andy Nicholas's ShrinkIt (1989), and
this program was checked against ShrinkIt 3.4 itself: it unpacks what
ShrinkIt packs, and ShrinkIt unpacks what it writes.
.PP
It takes its name and its option letters from Andy McFadden's
.B nulib2
and
.BR nulib ,
the reference NuFX implementations, which with
.I NufxLib
are distributed under the BSD license and were the second reader every
archive here was checked against.
What the format does and why is largely his documentation's account of
it \- the design of ShrinkIt's LZW, and the reason its chunk is 4096
bytes, come from lesson 9 of his
.IR "Hacking Data Compression" .
.PP
This is an independent implementation and shares no code or text with
any of them.
It means the same things by the option letters:
.B \-t
is that program's "quick dump of table of contents", not its
.B \-v
listing.
The columns
.B \-v
prints are maux's own \- they follow
.BR ls (1)'s
discipline rather than ShrinkIt's wider one \- but the letter carries
nulib2's meaning.
.TP
.B \-0
Store the files without compressing them.
The flag is about the WORK and not the bytes: coding 28 KB costs
about twenty seconds on a 1 MHz machine and storing it costs two.
.PP
Not implemented from the original:
.BR \-d ,
adding to an existing archive, reading an archive from standard
input, and every other modifier letter \-
.BR \-r ", " \-u ", " \-f ", " \-j ", " \-c ", " \-l ", " \-ll ", " \-s ", " \-e ", " \-ee ", " \-b .
.PP
.BR \-z " and " \-zz
are absent by design rather than unimplemented.
They select "deflate" and "bzip2", and the original's own help says
what that costs:
"these work much better than ShrinkIt's LZW, but the files can't be
unpacked on an Apple II".
An archive this machine cannot read is not one it should write.
.PP
.B \-b
is absent rather than unimplemented: it exists in the original to force
Binary II handling when an archive arrives on standard input and cannot
be seeked back over.
This program always has a file, so it simply looks, and a flag to make
it look would switch nothing.
.PP
Nothing WRITES a Binary II wrapper.
The original does not either \- its
.B \-b
is a reading modifier and is not valid on
.BR \-a .
ShrinkIt does, and says what for: a
.I .BXY
carries a ProDOS file type across a link that would lose it, for
uploading to a service whose own software cannot attach the header.
Archives leave this machine as disk images, which keep the type
already, so there is nothing here for such a wrapper to carry yet.
.PP
Two deliberate differences in
.BR \-i .
The original prints per-record progress and, on damage, ASKS
.RB \(lq "Archive may be damaged, continue anyway?" \(rq
\- a tool that blocks on input cannot be used in a pipeline.
This one is silent on success, names the record on failure, and never
prompts.
